    <h1 class="page-title">Introduction to CI/CD</h1>

    <div class="note-card">
      
        <h2>Why CI/CD?</h2>
        <p>For continuous integration and continuous deployment, Jenkins is the most widely used tool in the DevOps world. It automates the way code moves from a developer's laptop to a live production server.</p>

        <h2>CI &mdash; Continuous Integration</h2>
        <p>CI is the combination of <strong>continuous build + continuous test</strong>. Whenever a developer commits code using a source code management tool like GIT, the CI pipeline automatically picks up the change, builds it, and runs unit tests.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Since new code is integrated with old code immediately, we quickly know if the code is a success or failure.</li>
            <li>Errors are found much faster than manual testing.</li>
            <li>Products get delivered to the client more frequently.</li>
            <li>Developers don't need to perform manual build/test tasks.</li>
            <li>Overall it reduces developer effort by roughly 20% to 30%.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>CI Server</h3>
        <p>On a CI Server, Build, Test &amp; Deploy activities are all performed together on a single server.</p>
        <p class="formula">CI Server = Build + Test + Deploy</p>

        <h2>CD &mdash; Continuous Delivery / Deployment</h2>
        <h3>Continuous Delivery</h3>
        <p>Continuous Delivery makes a build artifact available for deployment. Whenever a new build artifact is ready, it is automatically placed in the desired environment. <strong>Deploying to production is still a manual step</strong> here &mdash; it needs human approval.</p>

        <h3>Continuous Deployment</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Every commit is automatically tested, built, and deployed straight to the production server.</li>
            <li>No manual approval is required.</li>
            <li>Deployment to production is fully automatic.</li>
            <li>In practice, around 99% of companies don't fully adopt this because of the risk involved.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The CI/CD Pipeline</h2>
        <p>The overall flow looks like a mini Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) with 6 phases:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Version Control</strong> &mdash; Developers commit code using GIT or SVN.</li>
            <li><strong>Build</strong> &mdash; The code gets compiled (e.g. using Maven for Java).</li>
            <li><strong>Unit Test</strong> &mdash; Code-level testing using tools like SonarQube / mvn test.</li>
            <li><strong>Deploy</strong> &mdash; Code is deployed to a Dev/Testing (pre-prod) environment.</li>
            <li><strong>Auto Test</strong> &mdash; Application-level automation testing using Selenium/JUnit.</li>
            <li><strong>Deploy to Production</strong> &mdash; If everything looks good, the code goes live.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>If an error occurs at any stage, feedback is given immediately and the issue is fixed before moving forward &mdash; this is what makes the entire SDLC automatic and fast when using Jenkins.</p>
        
    </div>

